# A top-level `let` bound to an annotated lambda whose body carries no class
# constraint. Nothing forced the parameters, so before the annotation was
# honoured this generalized to `forall a b. a -> b -> a` — MORE general than
# written — and `first(1, 2)` type-checked against a signature saying String.
#
# The annotation now pins the scheme to `String -> Int -> String`, so the call is
# the error it always should have been. This is the regression test for the one
# class of program whose meaning this change alters: compiles before and after,
# with a narrower inferred scheme.
module main

let first = \ (s: String, n: Int) -> s

fn main() -> Unit !{IO} = print(first(1, 2))
